  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider a cube such that the length of each side $s$ varies. What is the best interpretation of ${V}^{\u2032}(s)$ if $V$ is the volume of the cube?",
  "worked_solution": "The function $V(s)$ gives the volume of a cube with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}^{\u2032}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in\u00a0}V}{\\text{Change in\u00a0}s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${V}^{\u2032}(s)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as each side changes.",
